The Branch You Saw

Mentalism, orthogonal skills, and the design of an encounter
Pantheon × Money Bin · v1.0

Abstract

A mentalist can make an ordinary choice feel impossible by changing when an answer becomes available, how a choice is interpreted, or when its significance is revealed. The audience experiences one branch. The performer has access to a wider map.

Pantheon can use that distinction to build encounters that reward attention and replay. The Money Bin can use it to collect skills that each change a different part of an experience. This paper proposes a way to identify those skills, combine them, and test whether each one earns its place.

1. What the mentalist controls

Picture three coins on a table. The performer wants the middle coin to survive. “Touch a coin” leaves room to interpret the answer. If you touch the middle coin, the performer keeps it. If you touch an outside coin, the performer removes it and continues with the two remaining coins. A final instruction resolves those two in favor of the middle.

Your touch was real. The performer retained discretion over what your touch would do. That gap between the action you take and the consequence the system assigns it is the mechanism.

Other routines produce a similar feeling through different means.

Technique	What the performer controls	Pantheon use
Forcing	Which outcomes the procedure can reach	A shared scene approached through different routes
Secret peek	Who learns a fact and when	A character who can act on a clue before the player knows its significance
Multiple outs	Which prepared ending is revealed	Several endings that each make sense of the route taken
Cold reading	How a guess changes as feedback arrives	An adaptive narrator whose guesses remain visibly revisable
Hot reading	Which relevant facts are known before the encounter	A callback to a detail the participant knowingly supplied

A mentalism routine and an interactive story can look identical from the audience’s seat. Their contracts differ. A stage routine invites the audience to be fooled for fun. An interactive story earns replay when participants can inspect their choices and see what changed. For a consequential choice outside performance, make the available options and their effects clear before the person commits.

2. Orthogonal skills change different things

Here, orthogonal means two skills can be varied independently enough to see what each contributes. A stage magician and a filmmaker can both direct attention, so those professions may overlap on that dimension. Foley and translation can change different parts of the same scene. One can change what a door seems to weigh. The other can change who sounds entitled to open it.

Skill	What it changes	First experiment
Mentalism	Who knows what, which branches are possible, and when the answer appears	Run a three-coin routine. Write down every branch before performing it.
Foley	What an action seems to be made of or how much it weighs	Pair the same silent shot with three different impact sounds. Ask what changed in the object.
Film editing	What the viewer believes happened between shots	Put the same neutral reaction after a broken cup, then after an unopened letter. Ask what viewers inferred.
Improv	How the system incorporates an unexpected contribution	Let someone supply the object in a scene. Make one later event depend on that exact object.
Translation	Which meaning survives a change of language, register, or audience	Rewrite one invitation as a dare, a courtesy, and a summons. Keep the requested action identical.
Cartography	Which relationships become legible at a glance	Map one encounter by geography, then map it by who owes whom a favor. Compare the routes each map suggests.
Restoration	Which traces of prior use remain visible	Describe a repaired object twice. In one version reveal the repair. In the other, let one surviving mark carry its history.
Experimental design	Which explanation a trial can distinguish from its alternatives	Change one cue, record the expected effect before anyone tries it, and keep the misses.

A skill belongs in the Money Bin when it offers a repeatable move. “Use sound” is too broad to repeat. “Change the sound on an identical shot and ask what the object weighs” gives you a move you can test, teach, and reuse.

3. The encounter as a working unit

Start with a concrete encounter. Track the facts of the fictional world, the actions the participant can take, the cues they have received, and what they can later inspect. For each choice, write down what changes and what stays fixed. Then decide which part of the encounter each contributing skill controls.

This connects three existing Pantheon ideas:

* The Encounter Engine can hold the branch map and the consequences of each action.
* Semantic Surface Area can describe the places where meaning enters the scene. A word, sound, cut, silence, or remembered detail can each carry part of the same event.
* The Silent Guitar can hold possibilities the participant had to infer. On replay, one of those absent possibilities can become an experienced branch.

The writer can preserve the same underlying fact across multiple routes while giving each route its own evidence. A shared ending can still contain meaningful choices if those choices determine what the participant knows, who arrives with them, or what they can do next.

4. Demonstration — The Gate That Remembers

A traveler reaches a locked gate. Rain has filled the grooves of an inscription.

Anyone may enter twice.

The ground holds a bell, a key, and a blank slip of paper. The traveler can use one.

Choice	What happens	What the traveler carries through
Ring the bell	The gate opens. A second ring answers from behind the traveler.	Evidence that someone outside is listening.
Turn the key	The gate opens. Inside the lock is a fresh shaving of brass.	Evidence that the lock was recently altered.
Sign the paper	The gate opens. On the reverse is a second signature in the traveler’s hand.	Evidence of an earlier visit the traveler cannot remember.

Every path opens the gate. Each provides a distinct clue that can be used once inside. The bell can summon a witness. The shaving can persuade a locksmith to examine the gate. The second signature can get the traveler into a sealed records room. Those routes can lead to different accounts of the same first visit.

On replay, expose the three branches and ask which details the traveler remembers seeing, which they inferred, and which they learned only afterward. Then run the bell route twice with identical wording. In one version, let the answering bell sound distant. In the other, place it close behind the traveler. Ask where the unseen listener seemed to be. That isolates one contribution from Foley while keeping the branch itself fixed.

5. Money Bin — Three drills worth keeping

1. Same action, different meaning. Perform the coin routine for someone who has agreed to see a trick. Before the reveal, ask whether they thought touching a coin meant keeping it or removing it. Compare their answer to the full branch map. Save the exact instruction that left room for both readings.
2. Same scene, different surface. Run the gate scene with identical text and two different bell sounds. Ask each participant where the listener was and which cue led them there. Save both versions, including answers that go against your prediction.
3. Same ending, different consequence. Let two people take different routes through the gate. Give each the same problem inside and ask what they would do with the clue they received. If both can take the same actions for the same reasons, rewrite the clue or the next scene until their options genuinely diverge.

For each entry, keep the move, what changed, what stayed fixed, what someone actually did or reported, and where else the move might work. Preserve the original attempt beside revisions so a later reader can see which wording or cue produced which result.

The coin trick earns its reveal when you discover that your touch meant something different from what you assumed. The gate earns a second visit when you discover what another choice would have let you see, and what you could have done with it.